      "sequence_prompt": "Continue the exact incoming live-action shot for fifteen seconds without a cut or resetting the camera. Locke is the real adult man from <Picture 1>; Ultros is the single purple octopus from <Picture 2>. These two pictures maintain identity. The incoming moving footage determines their actual positions, clothing, lighting and set.\nThe high audience-side camera looks down onto the wooden gantry. Locke stands at screen left, holding the stair rail, with one boot on the landing. Ultros rests at screen right with an arm already extended along the floor toward Locke's boot. The pale wooden crossbar has already been knocked onto the deck and stays down. The horizontal grey iron counterweight remains roped securely at the back. Preserve these exact objects and damage. Warm diffuse work lighting stays constant.\nDuring the first four seconds the tip of the already extended arm curls around Locke's nearest boot and tugs it toward the step. Locke looks down at this contact, grips the solid stair rail and shifts his weight back. His foot slips only after the visible tug. His body remains connected to the handhold as he loses his balance.\nDuring seconds four through nine Locke's hand slides along the rail and he tumbles down the short flight beside him, toward screen left and downward. The camera follows that same visible stair route smoothly downward. Locke lands on a pile of thick folded canvas at the foot of the stairs. His knees and torso compress on landing and he rolls once onto the wooden stage floor. The fall is a short physical descent with continuous body motion and clear gravity.\nDuring seconds nine through fifteen Ultros follows down those same stairs. Two front arms reach and grip the lower steps before its heavy mantle moves off the gantry. The octopus lowers its weight step by step using those visible contacts, until it reaches the floor beside Locke. Locke plants a palm and one boot, rises into a guarded crouch and turns toward Ultro